course: biology
lesson: 114
title: Back to the wild: animals that zoos returned to nature
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some animals were wiped out in the wild and survived only in zoos, like the last books of a story kept safe in a library. When the danger outside was stopped, zoos could send them home again.
explain: The Arabian oryx, the European bison, Przewalski's horse, the California condor and the scimitar-horned oryx all came back this way, and tiny Partula snails went back to their islands in their thousands.
explain: Releasing is very hard: an animal born in a zoo does not know how to find food or spot a predator, so it first learns in a big pen in the wild, then wears a radio collar so scientists can see if it survives.
explain: That is why we cannot simply open all the cages: a release works only when the wild home is protected and the thing that killed the animals in the first place has gone.
fact: The Arabian oryx was wiped out in the wild by 1972 by hunting with cars and guns; zoos bred a «world herd» and released oryx in Oman from 1982, and it became the first animal to go from extinct in the wild to vulnerable.
fact: In 1987 the last wild California condors were caught, leaving 27 in the world; lead from bullets in carcasses was poisoning them, and today there are over 500, more than half flying wild.
fact: Przewalski's horses, short and stocky, are the last truly wild horses; they were extinct in the wild by the 1960s, and zoo-bred horses were released in Mongolia from the 1990s, where hundreds now live.
fact: Zoo-born animals may not know how to find food or avoid predators, so many first go through a soft release in big pens in the wild; golden lion tamarins survived better once they were released in family groups.
fact: European bison were wiped out in the wild by 1927, but about 50 lived in zoos; they were bred and released in Poland and other countries, there are now thousands, and a few were brought to Kent in England in 2022.
fact: Big grazers like bison eat, trample and rub against young trees, opening sunny clearings in a forest where other plants and animals can live.
fact: The scimitar-horned oryx was extinct in the wild by 2000; zoo-born oryx were released in Chad from 2016, and the herds now have calves in the wild.
fact: Some people think the oryx, seen from the side with its two horns in line, may have helped give rise to the unicorn legend, but no one can be sure.
fact: Released animals often wear radio collars or satellite tags, so scientists can see whether they find food, survive and have young.
fact: Some British zoos breed native animals like water voles (small river animals, not rats, that have vanished from much of Britain), sand lizards and pool frogs for release, and Chester Zoo and others have released thousands of water voles.
fact: Releases work only when the wild habitat is protected and the original danger is gone, and they cost a lot of money and years of work.
fact: Many zoos fund rangers and scientists in places like Kenya, because protecting animals where they live is often more effective than breeding them in zoos.
fact: Tiny Partula snails on Pacific islands such as Tahiti were wiped out by a predatory snail that people brought in to eat another pest snail; zoos bred them and have returned thousands.
exercise: What wiped out the Arabian oryx in the wild by 1972? | Hunting with cars and guns | A long drought only | A disease from zoo animals | Lions brought from Africa
exercise: Where were Arabian oryx first released again, from 1982? | Oman | Mongolia | Chad | Poland
exercise: Why does the Arabian oryx have a special place in conservation history? | It was the first animal to go from extinct in the wild to vulnerable | It was the first animal kept in a zoo | It was the first animal cloned | It was the first animal to fly
exercise: How many California condors were left in the world in 1987? | 27 | 2,700 | 7 | 270
exercise: What was poisoning California condors? | Lead from bullets in the carcasses they ate | Salt water from the sea | Fruit sprayed with pesticide | Smoke from forest fires only
exercise: Where were zoo-bred Przewalski's horses released from the 1990s? | Mongolia | Kenya | Australia | Brazil
exercise: What is a soft release? | Animals first live in big pens in the wild to learn | Animals are dropped from a plane | Animals are released on soft grass only | Animals are let out of a cage at night
exercise: What helped golden lion tamarins survive after release? | Releasing them in family groups | Releasing them one by one far apart | Painting them to look like leaves | Giving them collars with little bells
exercise: How many European bison were left in zoos when they died out in the wild? | About 50 | About 5,000 | Just 2 or 3 | None at all
exercise: Where in England were European bison brought in 2022? | Kent | London Zoo | The Lake District | Cornwall
exercise: Where were scimitar-horned oryx released from 2016? | Chad | Oman | Mongolia | Wyoming
exercise: Why do released animals wear radio collars? | So scientists can see if they find food, survive and have young | To keep them warm at night | To play music to them | To stop predators seeing or smelling them hiding in the long grass
exercise: Which animal have Chester Zoo and others released in thousands in Britain? | Water voles | Tigers | Penguins | Giraffes
exercise: Why can't we release all zoo animals? | Many could not survive, and many wild homes are gone or unsafe | Zoos want to keep the animals for money | All wild places are full already | Zoo animals are a different species
exercise: Why do some zoos pay for rangers in Kenya? | Protecting animals where they live is often more effective than zoo breeding | Rangers catch wild animals to sell them to big zoos around the world for money | It is the only way to get elephants | Kenya has no rangers of its own
exercise: What wiped out Partula snails on their islands? | A predatory snail people brought in to eat another pest snail | Hungry cats from passing ships | A huge storm | Tourists collecting them
test: «my mate says zoos just keep animals locked up and never let any go» What is true? | Zoos have returned oryx, bison, condors, horses and snails to the wild | Zoos are not allowed to release anything | Every zoo animal is released back into the wild after exactly one year there | Released zoo animals always die
test: What would happen if condors were released while ranchers still left lead bullets in carcasses? | Many condors would be poisoned again | The condors would learn to avoid bullets | The lead would make them fly much higher | Nothing, as lead is harmless to birds
test: Why can't oryx be released while people still hunt them from cars? | The original danger would wipe them out again | Oryx cannot run on sand | Cars are good for oryx | Oryx do not like living in the hot, dry desert at all
test: «unicorns were totally real, the oryx proves it» What is a fair answer? | An oryx seen from the side may have inspired the legend, but unicorns were not real | Yes, the oryx has a single magic horn | Yes, unicorns live in Chad today | No, the oryx has no horns at all
test: «a scimitar-horned oryx herd, 6 of 'em, how many horns» | 12, two horns each | 6, one horn each | 18, three horns each | 24, four horns each
test: In The Lion King, Simba grows up away from home and walks back as king. Why is it harder for a real zoo-born lion? | It never learnt to hunt or avoid danger in the wild | Real lions cannot walk such long distances on their own | Real lions do not have families | Real lions are scared of the sun
test: Why would a big grazing animal like the European bison help a forest? | Grazing and trampling open up clearings where other plants and animals can live | Bison plant new trees all over the forest by digging holes with their big curved horns | Bison eat all the insects | Bison keep the forest dark
test: «why bother breeding voles, they're just rats innit» What is the best answer? | They are native animals that vanished from much of Britain, and zoos help bring them back | Voles are exactly the same as brown rats, so breeding them is pointless and a waste of money | Voles are only bred to feed snakes | Voles are from Australia
test: «why'd they bring in a snail to eat snails, that's well dumb» What lesson did the Partula story teach? | Bringing in one animal to fix a problem can cause a bigger one | Snails always eat only pests | New animals brought in never cause any trouble on small islands | Islands need more predators
test: How would scientists know if a release worked? | Tracking shows the animals find food, survive and have young | Every animal comes back to the zoo | The animals send postcards | They count how many visitors came to see the release on the day
test: «3 condors and 2 Przewalski's horses released, how many legs» | 14, two for each condor and four for each horse | 20, four legs each | 10, two legs each | 12, two for each condor and three for each horse
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Arabian oryx. | It was extirpated in the wild by 1972 and reintroduced in Oman from 1982 | It has never been absent from the wild | It was reintroduced to Mongolia from zoo stock in 1927 | It was declared extinct in 2016 following its release in Chad
exam: Identify the principal condition that must be satisfied prior to the release of a captive-bred species. | The habitat must be protected and the original cause of decline removed | The species must first be exhibited for a period of ten years in no fewer than three zoos | The animals must have been born in the wild | The release must coincide with the opening of the zoo season
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a soft release? | Animals are first accommodated in large enclosures within the wild habitat in order to adapt | Animals are released without any preparation whatsoever | Animals are released only onto artificial grass | Animals are released solely during the hours of darkness so that they remain unseen by predators
exam: Ascertain the cause of the decline of Partula snails upon the islands of the Pacific. | Predation by a carnivorous snail introduced to control another pest species | The collection of shells by visiting tourists | The eruption of volcanoes upon every island | Competition for food with the water voles released upon the islands by British zoos
